title: Planner statistics
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: Exact per-field histograms kept as a derived key space, and a cost model that tells a scattered read from a contiguous one, so that the planner chooses from what an operand will read rather than from the query's shape.

The [[Query planner]] was built without statistics, on the belief that the cost
choices left were minor. The cold measurements of November 2026 showed otherwise
(user decision, 9 November 2026: build the statistics). This note is the design; it
is not built.

!! What showed it

[[The timed regression suite]]'s `--cold` pass (`cold.<scenario>`, the store evicted
from the page cache before each run) on the `L` size, 100 000 files:

|!Scenario |!Cold |
|`query.size_range` — `mfr_size > 900000` over the repository |80–92 ms |
|`query.size_range_in_folder` — the same range below `/dir0/dir1` |''1 270–1 390 ms'' |
|`query.subtree_range_text` — a subtree, `rating > 4` and a name pattern |''1 270–1 320 ms'' |
|`query.finder` |390–420 ms |
|`query.page`, `query.folder` — the cold floor of a first page |≈ 60 ms |

Bounding a range by a folder made it fifteen times ''slower''. Profiled
(`POST /repos/:repo/query/profile` on `L`):

|!Query |!Keys read |!Warm |!Cold |
|`mfr_size > 900000` |11 785 |1 ms |70 ms |
|`/dir0/dir1` (18 983 files) and `mfr_size > 900000` |''38 815'' |27 ms |''1 514 ms'' |

''Probe or walk'' (`index/plan.rs`, `probes`) probed: 18 983 candidates × `PROBE_COST`
(4) = 75 932 < 100 000 holders of `mfr_size`. The rule prices the walk at the field's
''holders'', which is the cost of a `!=` or a pattern — but a range walk reads only
the rows ''inside the range'', here 11 785. So the choice was wrong even counted in
keys, three times; and cold it was wrong by twenty, because a probe's keys are
scattered over the file (a candidate's rows, then the next one's) where a walk's are
contiguous (about a hundred per page). `query.subtree_range_text` is presumed to be
the same case (not yet profiled).

!! Why statistics rather than a bounded walk

The narrow repair is adaptive: walk the range, and switch to probing once the walk has
read `candidates × PROBE_COST` keys — never worse than twice the better side, no state
kept. It mends this one choice, after paying for part of the wrong side. Statistics
inform every choice the planner makes or could make:

* ''probe or walk'', decided before reading;
* ''the order of an `and`'' — today by shape only (bitmaps first, scans last, in the query's order); with cardinalities, the most selective operand first, each handed the intersection so far;
* ''fetch or walk'' for a sorted page ([[Sorting and postings]]), which already estimates, from little.

Choosing differently never answers differently: an `and` commutes, and a probe and a
walk decide each row by the same key (`tests/index_oracle.rs`). So the plan may depend on
the data without breaking a paginated session — the reason the order was kept a
property of the shape ([[How queries are evaluated]]: the cursor is tied to the
query sent; [[Query planner]]). That property must be restated, not lost: every page
gets the same ''results''; it may get them by another path if the data changed between
pages.

!! The statistics

''Exact, maintained like an index'' — a derived key space ([[Store tables]]), written
in each write's own transaction by `KvTxn::derive_row`, rebuilt by `reindex`, compared
by `check` — rather than sampled and refreshed, which would drift and make plans
depend on when the sample was taken.

* ''A histogram per ordered field'': a count of rows per ''bucket'', a bucket being a prefix of the value key (`kvstore/derived.rs`, `value_key`), so that buckets are contiguous ranges of the partition and a range's estimate is the sum of the buckets it covers, exact but for the two at its ends. The prefix width is per type, as each key encodes:
** a number is an order-preserving `f64` (`num_key`): its first 16 bits are the sign, the exponent and 4 bits of mantissa — buckets of 1/16 of an octave, log-scaled, which suits sizes and counts;
** a date is milliseconds in an `i64` (`dt_key`): the first 16 bits are 9 000 years wide, so a date bucket needs about the first 34 bits (2^30 ms, ≈ 12 days) — to settle;
** a text, its first two bytes; a tree name, likewise after the parent.
* ''Set sizes'' are mostly known exactly already: a field's holders (`present`), a posting, a subtree's descendants, a candidate set. They need no new key space, only to be read where the plan is made.

Only the populated buckets are stored: a key `field · bucket` → count. A row written or
removed changes one count: one key put more per row change of an ordered field.

!! The cost model

A cardinality is not a cost: the 38 815 keys above took 27 ms warm and 1.5 s cold. The
model prices a plan in ''contiguous'' and ''scattered'' key reads:

* a walk of a range: its estimated rows, contiguous;
* a probe: per candidate, a scattered read (its uuid, then its rows of the field) — the candidates are in dense-id order, so after a forest-order reindex the rows of one folder's records are close ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]), which a weight may later reflect;
* a bitmap: a key per 65 536 ids.

The weight of a scattered read against a contiguous one is calibrated with `--cold`,
not guessed; the warm weight is close to 1. One weight is used for both — the plan
should be good cold, where the time is; a weight that makes it worse warm is to be
measured, not assumed.

!! Steps

Each one ends with a commit, cost assertions by key count (`tests/kv_cost.rs`,
`KvStore::reads()`, never a duration — doc "Tests that fail under load") and a `--cold`
run on a neutral machine (the user's).

# ''The histogram'' as a derived key space: maintained in `derive_row`, rebuilt by `reindex`, compared by `check_derived`, `DERIVED_VERSION` bumped (a store of the previous format is reindexed when it opens — its migration, doc "Persisted format changes"). An estimate function `range_rows(field, op, value)`, tested against an exact count on the oracle's data. Measure `metarecord.write` and `tree.write` before and after.
# ''Probe or walk'' from the estimate and the cost model, replacing `PROBE_COST` against the holders for the ordered comparisons (`kvstore/source.rs`, `compare`; the text scan at `source.rs:740` keeps the holders, which a pattern does read). Test: the case above reads no more than the walk (`/dir0/dir1` and `mfr_size > 900000` on a generated repository: ≤ the range's keys plus the subtree's).
# ''The order of an `and''' by estimated selectivity, scans still last unless a scan's estimate is the smallest. Profile `query.subtree_range_text` and `query.finder` cold first, to know what they read.
# ''Re-measure what was "measured and not built"'' in [[Query planner]]: those conclusions were drawn warm, pricing a random read at ≈ 2 µs; cold, it is about a hundred times that.
# Update [[Query planner]] (statistics, the model, the restated determinism) and drop its "no statistics" — or merge this note into it.

!! Open

* The date bucket width, and whether a text histogram is worth its keys (a text range is rarer than a numeric one).
* Whether the probe weight should depend on how scattered the candidates are (one folder after a forest-order reindex, against ids spread over the repository).
* A field of mixed types (`int` and `string` rows): the buckets of each type tag are separate ranges already, since the tag leads the key.

!! How the measurements were taken

* `scripts/dev/bench.sh --cold --big` (`--filter query.` to narrow); the generated `L` lives in `target/bench-data/L`.
* Keys per phase: a daemon of its own on another port, `./target/release/metafolder-daemon --port 7631 --config crates/daemon/default-config/config.toml`, the requests authorised by `Authorization: Bearer $(cat $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/metafolder/daemon.token)`, then `POST /repos/load` and `POST /repos/:repo/query/profile` with the query's body. Cold by hand: unload, `fsync` and `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` on `.metafolder/internal/kv/data.mdb`, load, wait for `GET /repos/:repo` to say `ready`.
* Stop that daemon by its pid, never with `pkill -f` on a pattern the stopping command itself contains.
